from default_api import submit

submit(
    branch_name="bolt-kv-has-any",
    commit_message="⚡ Bolt: O(1) existence checks for KvSessionStore\n\nReplaced O(N) `load_all()` checks for existence with an O(1) `has_any()`\ncheck across `KvSessionStore` and `InMemorySessionStore`.\n\nWhy:\n`load_all()` in encrypted stores invokes heavy N+1 PBKDF2 operations just\nto verify if *any* items exist (used heavily in the relay setup flow).\n\nImpact:\nChecking if sessions exist no longer scales linearly with stored subjects,\nresulting in vastly improved performance on large, multi-user deployments.",
    description="**💡 What:**\nAdded a new `has_any()` method to both `KvSessionStore` and `InMemorySessionStore`. Refactored `relay_setup.py` to use `has_any()` instead of computing `len(store.load_all()) > 0`. Added a `.jules/bolt.md` entry.\n\n**🎯 Why:**\nIn encrypted Key-Value stores like `KvSessionStore`, `load_all()` performs massive I/O and CPU-bound decryption operations (PBKDF2) to load every single stored session into memory. Calling this function purely to check for the *existence* of any records (e.g., checking if the length is > 0) is a major performance bottleneck, especially on multi-user deployments with many active users.\n\n**📊 Impact:**\nChanges existence checks from O(N) (where N is the number of users times the cryptographic overhead) down to O(1) (simply reading the unencrypted `shared-index` array length). Drastically speeds up relay setup flow evaluation in large deployments.\n\n**🔬 Measurement:**\nUnit tests in `tests/auth/test_kv_session_store.py` and `tests/test_relay_setup.py` pass cleanly. The performance improvement can be measured directly via profiling on CF Workers or local deployments with hundreds of active `.session` records.",
    title="feat: ⚡ Bolt: [performance improvement] O(1) existence checks for KvSessionStore"
)
